comments.body:
Four key reasons for India's past tilt towards the West:

1. After its independence, India wisely chose English as one of its two official languages (Hindi is the other). That allowed much of India's elite to be educated in English, often in England.  Indeed talking and behaving like the ex-colonial masters became a way for India's elite to promote themselves;

2. Until July 1, 1997, when China officially got back Hong Kong from England, Indians - who got back their independence in August 15, 1947 after a revolt - looked down upon China as being backward, incapable of throwing off its colonial yoke.  That was 30 years ago and, too short for well-entrenched views to change;

3. Until recently, the US was the undisputed global military and economic power in Far East Asia. As such having ties with the US was economically and politically beneficial. Also, since its independence, India has been mired in territorial conflicts with its neighbors. With no seat in the UNSC, being politically close to the US made sense.

4. Statistically 2.3% of any population is highly intelligent. India, with its 1.48 billion people, has at least 30 million gifted individuals, many of whom born into India’s lower casts. They quickly realized improving their livelihood requires moving to countries where one’s religion or skin color is not barrier to progress. A good many of them moved to the US, taking over important positions in US industry and academia, creating strong ties between India and the US.
